[{"id":"wikiquote-8fd890737c9bd834df523288743917617acbb4bfe29c8134f940bd4c0a6c210c","text":"\"Your conjecture is totally wrong, I assure you. My mind was more agreeably engaged. I have been meditating on the very great pleasure which a pair of fine eyes in the face of a pretty woman can bestow.\"","pageTitle":"Pride and Prejudice","locator":"Darcy to Miss Bingley; Ch. 6","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3940795#Chapters_1-10","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-7b143fa5e760681ec67c0a3cd4be49870d60810c3e557ff62416302a0548f57d","text":"A lady's imagination is very rapid; it jumps from admiration to love, from love to matrimony, in a moment.","pageTitle":"Pride and Prejudice","locator":"Darcy to Miss Bingley, Ch. 6","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3940795#Chapters_1-10","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-cd86c20b0f24d5d5fb36c69a954e3376b55714f455b185696d3fb22d6987d94d","text":"\"Nothing is more deceitful,\" said Darcy, \"than the appearance of humility. It is often only carelessness of opinion, and sometimes an indirect boast.\"","pageTitle":"Pride and Prejudice","locator":"Ch. 10","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3940795#Chapters_1-10","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-a95b0e89dfc6d645029af208a2702a8dc53fad2018ab5a604a7726148c1154b9","text":"\"It is particularly incumbent on those who never change their opinion, to be secure of judging properly at first.\"","pageTitle":"Pride and Prejudice","locator":"Ch. 18","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3940795#Chapters_11-20","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-f4492ec18087e49510ee27fe7c7fea9da6589e87ee5d0031f0f5cb4fa7b991d1","text":"That will do extremely well, child. You have delighted us long enough. Let the other young ladies have time to exhibit.","pageTitle":"Pride and Prejudice","locator":"Ch. 18","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3940795#Chapters_11-20","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-b60e3c353b265f4dccfe85d3020cb19ce0c02f3117df83b5798967697ad4caff","text":"Next to being married, a girl likes to be crossed in love a little now and then. It is something to think of, and gives her a sort of distinction among her companions.","pageTitle":"Pride and Prejudice","locator":"Ch. 23","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3940795#Chapters_21-30","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-a573b8d76df91eda37b084338f1e39b4f9832c74b66bb214466b12912ee65801","text":"\"I like her appearance,\" said Elizabeth, struck with other ideas. \"She looks sickly and cross. Yes, she will do for him very well. She will make him a very proper wife.\"","pageTitle":"Pride and Prejudice","locator":"Ch. 28","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3940795#Chapters_21-30","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-a7b2b3b7d7017d1611d788ad4acd1e57fe1c70b0479577eb891740e89e9d87c2","text":"\"You have said quite enough, madam. I perfectly comprehend your feelings, and have now only to be ashamed of what my own have been. Forgive me for having taken up so much of your time, and accept my best wishes for your health and happiness.\"","pageTitle":"Pride and Prejudice","locator":"Ch. 34","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3940795#Chapters_31-40","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-81680b1cb2a4c8b3b284a569c336d4fb9a3790aa7e62bbb35ffbfd4000a63409","text":"\"There certainly was some great mismanagement in the education of those two young men. One has got all the goodness, and the other all the appearance of it.\"","pageTitle":"Pride and Prejudice","locator":"Ch. 40","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3940795#Chapters_31-40","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-153e89ca012794864e064b2a12dd75bb4c1fe933cb6d34f2b253cc1095550188","text":"\"Well, well,\" said he, \"do not make yourself unhappy. If you are a good girl for the next ten years, I will take you to a review at the end of them.\"","pageTitle":"Pride and Prejudice","locator":"Ch. 48","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3940795#Chapters_41-50","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-4d44f3c862d47b3d8db6331b69c63189a66a01f9af375c03a33c69d1657a046f","text":"Emma Woodhouse, handsome, clever, and rich, with a comfortable home and happy disposition, seemed to unite some of the best blessings of existence; and had lived nearly twenty-one years in the world with very little to distress or vex her.","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 1: The opening sentence.","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_I","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-2cb07f2a07dcca6bc9834c00c5aec201fba390d06d18680fb8848ac30093b3ba","text":"Where shall we see a better daughter, or a kinder sister, or a truer friend?","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 5: Mrs. Weston to Mr. Knightley, on Emma.","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_I","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-6f27846ade6b3b45c6bf427f8ff6c869af9f073ff2df84bd9cf981fdb40424f5","text":"Vanity working on a weak head, produces every sort of mischief.","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 8: Mr. Knightley to Mrs. Weston.","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_I","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-d0036ea9a16933b150b1fd67377086565d6f417e7b41b367d9cbc1501c88bdf7","text":"One half of the world cannot understand the pleasures of the other.","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 9: Emma to Mr. Woodhouse.","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_I","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-38d31fd7ccd2aab2c5ba322b0979bb83f1d5693235c3fe8c59b80bcd8b9808bd","text":"There are people, who the more you do for them, the less they will do for themselves.","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 11: Emma, thinking of Mr. Elton and Harriet Smith.","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_I","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-5469a8a69680cad21b5d711e42206efa5f09065a19303dec25b830337b5364cb","text":"Respect for right conduct is felt by every body.","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 18: Mr. Knightley to Emma.","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_I","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-ac9c0bf78a160be6e5e58f58a0c04e6febe9e241c904c1f00ad91a81cf55b899","text":"It is such a happiness when good people get together—and they always do.","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 3: Miss Bates.","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_2","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-5d1a11565e55d882ad7c9670e79041343e151b9158928d9a313d87e716095e85","text":"Harriet was one of those, who, having once begun, would be always in love.","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 4","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_2","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-4388e2e032f254118683e21db0339ef142528b2a6f30b7b4727fdf0e28ad0d6f","text":"Human nature is so well disposed towards those who are in interesting situations, that a young person, who either marries or dies, is sure of being kindly spoken of.","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 4: On the interest of the Highbury community in Augusta Hawkins (Mrs. Elton).","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_2","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-37e0c6a2dfc3084d257c4dce1bea58139f820be281018bc71cfa780a3b03d586","text":"What is right to be done cannot be done too soon.","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 5: Mr. Weston to Frank Churchill.","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_2","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-7d03895a0d02b4bd5710995be891741c2ce8cb477a7e4524edca6a78f7103c68","text":"There is safety in reserve, but no attraction. One cannot love a reserved person.","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 6: Frank Churchill to Emma.","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_2","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-4373be199f920fc84ff8a2cf12bc294247c488df65dd49168efcf13eddd002ce","text":"A mind lively and at ease, can do with seeing nothing, and can see nothing that does not answer.","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 9: Description of Emma observing the daily activities in Highbury.","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_2","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-b78ececa384e75d635f9c75ac51ec9b6e191c945debc054518f08129ef32f748","text":"I am very sorry to be right in this instance. I would much rather have been merry than wise.","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 12: Emma to Frank Churchill.","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_2","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-c830843911a61246697f8707f058d677d647812273f274e4c6184735359fb8cc","text":"Business, you know, may bring money, but friendship hardly ever does.","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 16: Mr. John Knightly to Jane Fairfax.","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_2","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-5b82c59c81853bfb6aab8204932418f5e1bd2fdfd84f102dfea31103bec98ec6","text":"Young ladies are delicate plants. They should take care of their health and their complexion.","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 16: Mr. Woodhouse to Jane Fairfax.","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_2","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-8d481273253ad1aa6d9e46011f5586177b3cd75064c7bc4ae8a7e4a83fab52f9","text":"Can you trust me with such flatterers?—Does my vain spirit ever tell me I am wrong?","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 2: Emma to Mr. Knightley, who is leaving her to \"her own reflections.\"","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_3","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-5c32d628ca90a160089b95308a7be83dd6b1d560c2d9304bb3f284d40eb2f150","text":"General benevolence, but not general friendship, made a man what he ought to be.","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 2: Emma, thinking critically of Mr. Weston.","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_3","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-422ca86a5e04e25f986569ee43ff8ef87bf2fbf5ce26d8d2cf3151ef49c66000","text":"Seldom, very seldom, does complete truth belong to any human disclosure; seldom can it happen that something is not a little disguised, or a little mistaken; but where, as in this case, though the conduct is mistaken, the feelings are not, it may not be very material.","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 13: On Emma's response to Mr. Knightley's proposal.","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_3","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-f80f6ed8b4dab605682c4e0afb5745db54741dcc31033b5bc5ab703eaa7d5d07","text":"It is very difficult for the prosperous to be humble.","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 14: Frank Churchill to Mrs. Weston, in a letter.","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_3","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-f7c6f1bda3582f3255b52d5b54c2304d461052a7ada9c7880cce77fd5e4e6f2d","text":"One man’s style must not be the rule of another’s.","pageTitle":"Emma (novel)","locator":"Chapter 15:: Mr. Knightley to Emma.","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952621#Volume_3","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-d1f23a1c2a612ab9db568b931b25fe552667cef9cc0836ba311d7267171684a4","text":"The bitter past, more welcome is the sweet.","pageTitle":"All's Well That Ends Well","locator":"King of France, scene iii","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3027604#Act_V","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-d4a8d59f99dc365e64f55e52ee64419c1286091e7ca1f590ee17f791c9a232c8","text":"I no further offend you than becomes me for my good.","pageTitle":"As You Like It","locator":"Orlando, l. 71","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3756884#Scene_i","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-f223b0bfacab4aedb5ff9e69afb1d90aec3cb905d912ddc2fca5a54ebdab4fc2","text":"Now will I stir this gamester.","pageTitle":"As You Like It","locator":"Oliver, l. 145","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3756884#Scene_i","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-e6f72db64b8372c985b029f9af7d93437b51cace28acb6fa8d63fe4a79788efb","text":"If I had a thunderbolt in mine eye, I can tell who should down.","pageTitle":"As You Like It","locator":"Celia, l. 193","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3756884#Scene_ii","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-89dbeaa69994610672c6bc04dfcde8463490feadb95981f7bda0a7e0936d83a9","text":"My pride fell with my fortunes.","pageTitle":"As You Like It","locator":"Rosalind, l. 231","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3756884#Scene_ii","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-aeb1122cdb1067d9145b0372715f90efd7f90cd51cc1f44fd5e4ae141041cf80","text":"I shall ne’er be ware of mine own wit till I break my shins against it.","pageTitle":"As You Like It","locator":"Touchstone, l. 54","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3756884#Scene_iv","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-1fe37025be8f247a8252d6e80a53caeca395119c9aa038b5bd37338f0e995b14","text":"I am ambitious for a motley coat.","pageTitle":"As You Like It","locator":"Jaques, l. 43","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3756884#Scene_vii","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-249cb5372c35ee80013d9ca5467e461143f312e86f794eb47f5a92395264be4a","text":"Sit down and feed, and welcome to our table.","pageTitle":"As You Like It","locator":"Duke Senior, l. 105","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3756884#Scene_vii","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-4fac61b1a2022ad9f7bb1c7c8c0449737ab6fbed109874742936ca1101faa81f","text":"Those that are good manners at the court are as ridiculous in the country as the behaviour of the country is most mockable at the court.","pageTitle":"As You Like It","locator":"Corin, l. 41","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3756884#Scene_ii","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-aa3211de0330ded52fecd33990a71434ae877ae216fd124b70c794810d5e0954","text":"This is the very false gallop of verses.","pageTitle":"As You Like It","locator":"Touchstone, l. 103","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3756884#Scene_ii","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-26ec0417db53957b75c244423d3ae67c006e05ee76a5c1ec1f8e2680db3c7d50","text":"Answer me in one word.","pageTitle":"As You Like It","locator":"Rosalind, l. 209","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3756884#Scene_ii","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-8eeccbc73163b780a0bf6e87e999829fed2ce3d89802ac266aaf44bef27523b5","text":"I do desire we may be better strangers.","pageTitle":"As You Like It","locator":"Orlando, l. 243","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3756884#Scene_ii","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-65f3c51cc1c20edd72d48ab63baac0a38701b6c9de7e73c1a26a24908dc7c47f","text":"I will chide no breather in the world but myself, against whom I know most faults.","pageTitle":"As You Like It","locator":"Orlando, l. 263","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3756884#Scene_ii","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-4e5c7e00be3566fa2fd1e286d2c65092b8b5a35126e672ab43a96d9de4ed4939","text":"And his kissing is as full of sanctity as the touch of holy bread.","pageTitle":"As You Like It","locator":"Rosalind, l. 12","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3756884#Scene_iv","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-003005a7c06699dd907b9bed367a6cbae3ed3a57e4220738867d1253f30d6ecc","text":"I have gained my experience.","pageTitle":"As You Like It","locator":"Jaques,  l. 23","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3756884#Scene_i","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-ce104b0e2533a4945be346fca1e42227a96e9e5a1e2149067edae0b87feb991c","text":"I will scarce think you have swam in a gondola.","pageTitle":"As You Like It","locator":"Rosalind, l. 33","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3756884#Scene_i","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-b3ff38531f4370fbe36391df07732d1f69bfab3729d7159cc7c7cdee4425c798","text":"Men have died from time to time and worms have eaten them, but not for love.","pageTitle":"As You Like It","locator":"Rosalind, l. 94","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3756884#Scene_i","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-cb08c86715fdafe8e4153453041c7e2fa3bf9b70dc0d7734bd493af449126f8f","text":"Here comes a pair of very strange beasts, which in all tongues are called fools.","pageTitle":"As You Like It","locator":"Jaques, l. 36","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3756884#Scene_iv","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-349cc4fe5cf78266e01f232eb080afed06b8f53bc7f2bbc69fb24282365d0d45","text":"If it be true that good wine needs no bush, ’tis true that a good play needs no epilogue","pageTitle":"As You Like It","locator":"Rosalind, l. 3","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3756884#Epilogue","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-a76978b57e4c003d0dd813c08c89b0cc29ca870d68df1ada4476b1700e08b31c","text":"Shall we shog?","pageTitle":"Henry V (play)","locator":"Nym, l. 43","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3953658#Scene_iii","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-6e1a44443fa6ed2c380c7bf5d939f76bbef809ffa0904a84ae963502a8dc8b7a","text":"I would give all my fame for a pot of ale and safety.","pageTitle":"Henry V (play)","locator":"Boy, l. 10","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3953658#Scene_ii","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-ab025328671ec47a806c32be0769a60fc13e58de443659664f246d25cd5517fa","text":"When I bestride him, I soar, I am a hawk.","pageTitle":"Henry V (play)","locator":"Orleans, l. 14","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3953658#Scene_vii","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-bf7ccb43475721429df79ea732f39246883b48919af6b6a39a26006f4421c914","text":"There is occasions and causes why and wherefore in all things.","pageTitle":"Henry V (play)","locator":"Fluellen, l. 3","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3953658#Scene_i","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-669fbd47ff0248bf7a6fd6adee3c887b8c06162a4fc6a3f59dba8ba7489256f2","text":"The mirror of all courtesy.","pageTitle":"Henry VIII (play)","locator":"2 Gentleman, scene i","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3027658#Act_II","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-f1926fd1eba995552a658dd23fe4be11284f926acc1c77050f95fc7d5b2ec076","text":"This bold bad man.","pageTitle":"Henry VIII (play)","locator":"Lord Chamberlain, scene ii","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3027658#Act_II","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-a596029a08cc5d317664527119298b2c41ef25bcac93260111e77038c2b91007","text":"Press not a falling man too far!","pageTitle":"Henry VIII (play)","locator":"Lord Chamberlain, scene ii","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3027658#Act_III","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-b4952c68516457ee37da1c723f6b8f0ff60f4e052b3c78148e04da5e2651edda","text":"A royal train, believe me.","pageTitle":"Henry VIII (play)","locator":"2 Gentleman, scene i","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3027658#Act_IV","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-a34a263bab441615bb7b5f2a9b9e1140eb817eee7d807befb7f26d1363b6b320","text":"Some rise by sin, and some by virtue fall.","pageTitle":"Measure for Measure","locator":"Escalus, scene i","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3922582#Act_II","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-5dbf868d0524346adf776ca4123698c6f5225bc027c5c32fa4d392ea17a23d94","text":"Condemn the fault, and not the actor of it?","pageTitle":"Measure for Measure","locator":"Angelo, scene ii","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3922582#Act_II","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-c7df1c1ba3392f5b36fe9d815b107cd74ef20be665493ee52f32f3d4a31617fd","text":"Virtue is bold, and goodness never fearful.","pageTitle":"Measure for Measure","locator":"Duke Vincentio, scene i","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3922582#Act_III","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-0625890e3b514016bcf1770fa026d14cc2817aa6228780fee277288a9c8aea18","text":"Every true man’s apparel fits your thief.","pageTitle":"Measure for Measure","locator":"Abhorson, scene ii","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3922582#Act_IV","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-278a628ea60c11ee08f2d14eaed4357e4e701fbb5baad5dfd160dfd8a8e357c1","text":"What’s mine is yours, and what is yours is mine.","pageTitle":"Measure for Measure","locator":"Duke Vincentio, scene i","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3922582#Act_V","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-a7b1392b576d10c3bcd967f5c33ce00537d6b92675219efa8b1afeedb342a651","text":"If to do were as easy as to know what were good to do, chapels had been churches, and poor men’s cottages princes’ palaces.","pageTitle":"The Merchant of Venice","locator":"Portia, l. 10","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952634#Scene_ii","rightsEvidence":{}},{"id":"wikiquote-536031afd41a21d48dbf6882e6bc6df3c9f381bb580b6c910ea7b40eb90c7b45","text":"I fear he will prove the weeping philosopher when he grows old, being so full of unmannerly sadness in his youth.","pageTitle":"The Merchant of Venice","locator":"Portia, l. 45","url":"https://en.wikiquote.org/w/index.php?oldid=3952634#Scene_ii","rightsEvidence":{}}]
